          up: {"title":"Name the category (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A language in Trinidad is now spoken mainly by elderly people in a few villages. It spread in the late 1700s, when French-speaking planters and the Africans they enslaved settled on the island, and most of its words come from French. It is BEST described as...","options":[{"t":"a dialect of French","fb":"Its vocabulary is mainly French, but its grammar developed through contact and differs systematically from French. It is a creole, not a dialect of French."},{"t":"an indigenous language, because it has been spoken in Trinidad for over two hundred years","fb":"Indigenous languages belong to the peoples who were in the region before European contact. This one arose from colonial contact."},{"t":"a pidgin, because so few people now speak it","fb":"The number of speakers does not decide the category. It had native speakers for generations, so it is a creole, though an endangered one."},{"t":"an endangered French-lexified creole","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It arose from contact and became a native language (a creole), its words come mainly from French (its lexifier), and it is spoken mostly by older people (endangered)."}]}
