          up: {"title":"The job application email (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jaden, 18, is applying for a summer internship at a Bridgetown law firm. This time every stage of the exchange tests his digital register, including a reply that is less formal than he expected.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Jaden has a professional address ready. Which <b>subject line</b> should he use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"URGENT!!! Internship\"","to":"bad2","fb":"Capitals and exclamation marks read as shouting and pressure."},{"t":"\"Hi\"","to":"bad1","fb":"A vague subject line may be ignored or filtered as spam."},{"t":"\"Application: Summer Internship – Jaden Richardson\"","to":"n2","fb":"Clear and professional: it states the purpose and his name."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Which paragraph best explains why he is suitable?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"I really need this job bad. I'm hard-working and stuff, and I learn quick.\"","to":"bad3","fb":"The casual register and vague claims undercut the message."},{"t":"\"I am a sixth-form student in Bridgetown studying Law and Communication Studies. Last summer I volunteered at the Community Legal Aid Clinic, where I filed documents and greeted clients. I would welcome the chance to develop these skills at your firm.\"","to":"n3","fb":"Concise, specific and formal, with relevant experience."},{"t":"A 400-word paragraph about his childhood dream of becoming a lawyer, his favourite television dramas and his family history.","to":"ok1","fb":"Sincere, but far too long and mostly irrelevant. Busy readers want concise evidence of suitability."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The firm's office manager replies: \"Hi Jaden! Thanks for this :) Could you send us a reference letter?\" How should Jaden reply?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Dear Ms. Arrindell, Thank you for your reply. Please find my reference letter attached. Kind regards, Jaden Richardson\"","to":"n4","fb":"Polite and professional. He can be warm without dropping his register: the applicant should not relax more than the employer."},{"t":"\"Hi!! Sure thing :) sending now\"","to":"ok2","fb":"Converging completely with her casual tone feels friendly, but as the applicant, Jaden risks seeming unprofessional."},{"t":"Wait three days, then send the letter without a message.","to":"bad4","fb":"A slow reply with no message suggests a lack of interest and courtesy."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Before sending, Jaden notices that his attachment is named \"ref letter FINAL final2.docx\" and that his voicemail greeting is a dancehall track with no message. What does he do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Rename the file \"Jaden_Richardson_Reference.pdf\" and record a short, professional voicemail greeting.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. File names and voicemail are digital identifiers too, and the firm will see or hear both."},{"t":"Leave both: the content of the letter is what matters.","to":"bad5","fb":"Small digital details shape first impressions. An untidy file name and a music voicemail suggest carelessness."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Interview offered!</b> Jaden used a clear subject line, a concise formal paragraph, a reply that was warm but professional, and tidy digital identifiers. He showed that register applies to every part of a digital exchange.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed result.</b> The office manager skims the long paragraph and cannot quickly find his experience. He may still be considered, but concise, relevant writing would have served him better.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed result.</b> The office manager does not mind, but a partner who reads the thread wonders whether Jaden can write formally to clients. Mirror an employer's warmth, not their informality.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost in the inbox.</b> The email is filed with spam and never read. A subject line should state the purpose and your name. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong impression.</b> Capitals and exclamation marks make the email seem demanding before it is even opened. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Unconvincing.</b> Casual phrasing and vague claims such as \"and stuff\" suggest that Jaden has not taken the application seriously. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Opportunity missed.</b> By the time the letter arrives, the internship has been offered to someone who replied promptly. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Careless details.</b> The partner notices the file name and hears the music when she calls. She decides to interview a more professional candidate. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
